🚀 The feature, motivation and pitch
2 techniques commonly used to reduce GPU memory footprint are checkpointing of activations within an nn.Module, and offloading intermediate activations between successively checkpointing modules to CPU so that they are not stored on GPU.
With [https://pytorch.org/docs/master/autograd.html?highlight=saved_tensors_hooks#torch.autograd.graph.saved_tensors_hooks](saved tensor hooks), we can implement both of these features, but these hooks also enable us to take a more "intelligent" approach to combine these 2 techniques.
Concretely, given a tensor x, we could define some custom logic (or a user-provided lambda) to determine whether x should be checkpointed (discarded and recomputed in backward pass), or offloaded to CPU.
Concretely, this could look something like:
def pack(x):
    if should_offload(x):
        return (True, x.device, x.to("cpu"))
    return (False, index(x))

def unpack(x):
    offloaded = x[0]
    if offloaded:
        x = x[2].to(x[1])
    else:
         return storage[x[1]]

Additional discussion is needed as to how to determine whether x should be checkpointed or offloaded. It is possible that the tensor is not enough information and we may need access to the module that produced x (i.e. if it is expensive to recompute, we may want to offload it, if tensor is very large but fast to recompute, we might want to checkpoint it).
